1
00:00:05,018 --> 00:00:10,743
This is Tendero: an AI sales agent that sells, collects and reconciles payments with PayPal,

2
00:00:10,783 --> 00:00:12,998
for small shops that sell over chat.

3
00:00:13,425 --> 00:00:16,553
Across Latin America, small shops sell through chat.

4
00:00:16,593 --> 00:00:20,038
The owner answers every message by hand, sends payment links,

5
00:00:20,078 --> 00:00:22,837
and guesses which payment belongs to which order.

6
00:00:22,877 --> 00:00:27,378
Sales get lost at night, and fake 'I already paid' screenshots slip through.

7
00:00:27,784 --> 00:00:31,398
On the left, a customer of a mate workshop in Buenos Aires.

8
00:00:31,438 --> 00:00:33,412
On the right, the owner's console.

9
00:00:33,452 --> 00:00:37,670
Here the agents run on the offline demo brain; with an API key,

10
00:00:37,710 --> 00:00:39,630
Claude drives the same tools.

11
00:00:40,253 --> 00:00:41,737
Lucía writes from Madrid.

12
00:00:41,777 --> 00:00:45,267
The agent calls tools to price the cart and answer about shipping.

13
00:00:45,307 --> 00:00:48,199
Prices come from the server, never from the model.

14
00:00:48,612 --> 00:00:52,272
Only after she confirms the total can the agent create a PayPal order.

15
00:00:52,312 --> 00:00:55,412
That guardrail lives in code, not in the prompt.

16
00:00:55,815 --> 00:01:01,500
Orders and refunds go through the official PayPal Server SDK, generated by APIMatic.

17
00:01:01,540 --> 00:01:06,990
This demo points it at a local sandbox simulator; with sandbox keys, it talks to PayPal.

18
00:01:07,409 --> 00:01:10,780
Tendero captures the order and PayPal sends a webhook.

19
00:01:10,820 --> 00:01:15,537
Tendero has PayPal verify the signature, reconciles amount and currency,

20
00:01:15,577 --> 00:01:20,031
records the fee and the net, and only then tells Lucía she has paid.

21
00:01:20,440 --> 00:01:21,635
What if someone fakes it?

22
00:01:21,675 --> 00:01:27,321
A forged 'payment completed' webhook fails signature verification, and no order changes.

23
00:01:27,722 --> 00:01:32,414
Once the payment is verified, the work is booked on the workshop's fulfillment board,

24
00:01:32,454 --> 00:01:36,255
built with Bryntum Scheduler: quality check, packing, courier pickup.

25
00:01:36,295 --> 00:01:38,303
Lucía already got her dispatch day.

26
00:01:38,343 --> 00:01:42,471
If the owner drags the pickup to the wrong time, the server refuses:

27
00:01:42,511 --> 00:01:44,371
the courier only comes at five.

28
00:01:44,784 --> 00:01:47,993
The owner's copilot drives the AG Grid ledger.

29
00:01:48,033 --> 00:01:52,368
Show me unpaid orders over twenty dollars, and the grid filters itself.

30
00:01:52,408 --> 00:01:56,607
Inventory updates from verified payments, with AG Charts.

31
00:01:57,003 --> 00:01:58,429
Are our prices competitive?

32
00:01:58,469 --> 00:02:02,422
The copilot asks Channel3's product search for comparable products.

33
00:02:02,462 --> 00:02:07,206
Here it replays a sample response; with an API key the search is live.

34
00:02:07,628 --> 00:02:08,661
How did we do today?

35
00:02:08,701 --> 00:02:14,405
Real numbers from the ledger: gross, PayPal fees, net, and the fake webhook it blocked.

36
00:02:15,300 --> 00:02:18,034
Refund the last order, the gourd arrived cracked.

37
00:02:18,074 --> 00:02:23,179
The agent issues a PayPal refund, the signed webhook confirms it, the customer is told,

38
00:02:23,219 --> 00:02:25,722
and the order leaves the fulfillment board.

39
00:02:26,440 --> 00:02:31,877
Under the hood: a provider-agnostic tool loop on Claude Sonnet, the PayPal Server SDK,

40
00:02:31,917 --> 00:02:36,654
webhook verification, an idempotent ledger, and thirty-six automated tests.

41
00:02:37,050 --> 00:02:42,191
Tendero is open source under the MIT license and runs locally in five minutes.

42
00:02:42,231 --> 00:02:45,308
Sell while you sleep, and know every payment is real.

